    <p>
      <strong style="color:var(--text);">2020–2026 in Context:</strong>
      The 2020s have so far averaged <span class="highlight">$71.75/bbl</span> across 75 months (through March 2026),
      making this the second-highest average decade in nominal terms. The COVID trough of $21.04 (April 2020) and
      the Ukraine-war peak of $116.80 (June 2022) bookend a period of exceptional realized volatility.
      Since mid-2022, prices have progressively normalized to a range of $57–92/bbl, though the
      <span class="highlight">March 2026 surge to $87.84</span> (+36.2% from February's $64.47) introduces new uncertainty.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong style="color:var(--text);">2025 Softness:</strong>
      The full year 2025 (January–December, 12 observations) averaged approximately
      <span class="highlight">$63.55/bbl</span>, the lowest annual average since 2020 and reflective of
      demand-side pressure from slowing global growth and rising non-OPEC supply. The December 2025
      price of $57.95/bbl was the lowest reading since April 2021.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong style="color:var(--text);">Structural Observations:</strong>
      Five structural observations emerge from the full 56-year dataset:
    </p>
    <p>
      (1) <span class="highlight">Regime persistence:</span> Crude oil tends to spend extended periods (3–8 years) within a price band
      before a structural break — gradual change is not the norm. (2) <span class="highlight">Mean reversion is weak:</span>
      Each new price regime establishes a higher nominal floor than the prior one, consistent with a long-run upward drift
      (CAGR 7.93%). (3) <span class="highlight">Volatility clustering:</span> Large monthly moves cluster around identifiable
      macro events and then subside — rolling volatility is not uniformly distributed.
      (4) <span class="highlight">Geopolitical risk premium:</span> Supply disruptions (embargoes, wars, sanctions) have historically
      triggered larger price spikes than demand shocks — which tend to produce slower, more gradual moves unless accompanied
      by a financial crisis. (5) <span class="highlight">Nominal vs. real:</span> All figures here are nominal USD;
      in inflation-adjusted terms, the 1980 peak (~$40/bbl nominal) would likely exceed even the 2008 peak in real terms,
      suggesting the supercycle narrative should be tempered when adjusting for purchasing power.
    </p>
  </div>

